1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,018
This is ECE 320, video 9. The topic is data hazards and forwarding.

2
00:00:05,468 --> 00:00:12,713
After this video, you can find all RAW, WAW and WAR pairs in a code sequence.

3
00:00:13,163 --> 00:00:21,290
You can decide how each value reaches the instruction that uses it: by an MX, WX or WM forward, or after a stall.

4
00:00:21,740 --> 00:00:25,687
You can fill the forwarding table and the stall table of an exam diagram.

5
00:00:26,137 --> 00:00:29,388
And you can calculate the CPI with load-use stalls.

6
00:00:29,838 --> 00:00:34,383
Slide 15 defines a dependence: a relation between two instructions.

7
00:00:34,833 --> 00:00:42,774
In a data dependence, both instructions use the same storage location. The add writes x3, and the subtract reads x3.

8
00:00:43,224 --> 00:00:47,450
In a control dependence, one instruction decides if the other executes.

9
00:00:47,900 --> 00:00:54,588
In a single-cycle processor, the add ends before the subtract starts. So the older instruction always acts first.

10
00:00:55,038 --> 00:01:01,446
In a pipeline, the two instructions overlap. The add writes x3 in W, in cycle 5.

11
00:01:01,896 --> 00:01:07,562
But the subtract reads x3 in D, in cycle 3. That is before the write in cycle 5.

12
00:01:08,012 --> 00:01:16,487
A hazard is a dependence plus the chance of a wrong order. The pipeline must make sure that the effect of a wrong order is never visible outside.

13
00:01:16,937 --> 00:01:28,082
Slide 22 names three kinds of data dependence. The first is read after write, or RAW. The older instruction writes r3, and the younger one reads r3.

14
00:01:28,532 --> 00:01:32,526
The younger instruction reads r3 in D, in cycle 3.

15
00:01:32,976 --> 00:01:40,964
The older instruction writes r3 in W, in cycle 5. The read comes before the write. So RAW is a hazard.

16
00:01:41,414 --> 00:01:48,380
The second is write after read, or WAR. The older instruction reads r1, and the younger one writes r1.

17
00:01:48,830 --> 00:01:55,145
The read is in cycle 2, and the write is in cycle 6. The read comes first, so the order is kept.

18
00:01:55,595 --> 00:02:01,029
The third is write after write, or WAW. Both instructions write r3.

19
00:02:01,479 --> 00:02:05,798
Both write in W, in program order: in cycles 5 and 6. The order is kept.

20
00:02:06,248 --> 00:02:11,349
So in our in-order 5-stage pipeline, only RAW causes a problem.

21
00:02:11,799 --> 00:02:16,350
Later in this video, a long multiply makes WAW a problem too.

22
00:02:16,800 --> 00:02:30,175
Now a midterm question: fall 2019, question 1 part B. Find all RAW, WAW and WAR pairs in these four instructions. Pause the video and try it.

23
00:02:30,625 --> 00:02:38,729
First, list the reads and writes of each instruction. Instruction 1, the add, reads x1 and x3, and writes x1.

24
00:02:39,179 --> 00:02:43,870
Instruction 2, the load word, reads x2 and writes x1.

25
00:02:44,320 --> 00:02:50,589
Instruction 3, the store word, reads x1 and x3. It writes no register.

26
00:02:51,039 --> 00:02:56,751
Instruction 4, the subtract, reads x2 and x1, and writes x4.

27
00:02:57,201 --> 00:03:01,009
Now the RAW pairs. Instructions 3 and 4 read x1.

28
00:03:01,459 --> 00:03:07,682
Their most recent older writer of x1 is instruction 2. So the RAW pairs are 2 3 and 2 4.

29
00:03:08,132 --> 00:03:14,494
The pairs 1 3 and 1 4 are not RAW pairs, because instruction 2 writes x1 in between.

30
00:03:14,944 --> 00:03:22,421
Now the WAW pairs. Instructions 1 and 2 both write x1. So 1 2 is a WAW pair.

31
00:03:22,871 --> 00:03:32,484
Now the WAR pairs. Instruction 1 reads x1, and then instruction 2 writes x1. So 1 2 is also a WAR pair.

32
00:03:32,934 --> 00:03:45,489
The answer: RAW 2 3 and 2 4, WAW 1 2, and WAR 1 2.

33
00:03:45,939 --> 00:03:55,367
Slide 16 asks a question. The or instruction does not use the data memory. Could it skip M, and reach W in cycle 5?

34
00:03:55,817 --> 00:03:58,882
The add before it is also in W in cycle 5.

35
00:03:59,332 --> 00:04:07,830
Both need the register-file write port in the same cycle. This is a structural hazard: two instructions use one hardware resource at the same time.

36
00:04:08,280 --> 00:04:15,664
There are two fixes. Fix 1: the instructions take turns, so some must stall.

37
00:04:16,114 --> 00:04:22,987
Fix 2: add more hardware. This always works. ISAs are designed to avoid structural hazards.

38
00:04:23,437 --> 00:04:32,679
Our pipeline uses a simple rule: every instruction goes through all 5 stages. So the or passes M in cycle 5, and writes in W in cycle 6.

39
00:04:33,129 --> 00:04:40,002
Now the add writes in cycle 5, and the or writes in cycle 6. One instruction still finishes per cycle.

40
00:04:40,452 --> 00:04:50,344
Slide 18 looks at the register file. In D, an instruction reads up to two registers: rs1 by read port A, and rs2 by read port B.

41
00:04:50,794 --> 00:04:56,168
In W, an older instruction writes one register, rd, by the write port.

42
00:04:56,618 --> 00:05:04,931
So the register file has two read ports and one write port. All 3 accesses can happen in the same cycle. There is no structural hazard.

43
00:05:05,381 --> 00:05:11,607
Slide 19 looks at memory. Assume one memory for instructions and data. Here are four instructions.

44
00:05:12,057 --> 00:05:17,073
In cycle 4, the load word is in M. It reads data from the memory.

45
00:05:17,523 --> 00:05:27,879
In the same cycle, the store word is in F. It reads its instruction from the same memory. Two accesses to one memory in one cycle: a structural hazard.

46
00:05:28,329 --> 00:05:35,756
Slide 20 gives three solutions. Solution 1: separate memories for instructions and data, or separate caches.

47
00:05:36,206 --> 00:05:42,150
Solution 2: one memory with 2 accesses per cycle. It is slow and expensive.

48
00:05:42,600 --> 00:05:44,543
Solution 3: a stall.

49
00:05:44,993 --> 00:05:49,777
Our pipeline uses solution 1: IMEM for instructions, and DMEM for data.

50
00:05:50,227 --> 00:05:58,493
Real programs pass values through registers. Slide 24 asks if this sequence executes correctly. The add writes x3.

51
00:05:58,943 --> 00:06:01,451
All three later instructions read x3.

52
00:06:01,901 --> 00:06:06,836
The add writes x3 into the register file in its W stage, in cycle 5.

53
00:06:07,286 --> 00:06:14,298
The load word reads x3 in D, in cycle 3. That is 2 cycles before the write. It gets the wrong value.

54
00:06:14,748 --> 00:06:22,689
The add immediate reads x3 in D, in cycle 4. That is 1 cycle before the write. It also gets the wrong value.

55
00:06:23,139 --> 00:06:34,471
The store word reads x3 in D in cycle 5, the same cycle as the write. The slide says: maybe, but not in our case. D sees only writes of earlier cycles.

56
00:06:34,921 --> 00:06:44,487
Slide 26 asks about memory data hazards. A load word follows a store word to the same address, x3. Does the load get the right value?

57
00:06:44,937 --> 00:06:48,281
The store writes the data memory in M, in cycle 4.

58
00:06:48,731 --> 00:06:52,446
The load reads the data memory in M, in cycle 5.

59
00:06:52,896 --> 00:06:58,980
Both accesses are in the same stage, M. So they stay in program order. There is no memory data hazard.

60
00:06:59,430 --> 00:07:06,117
Registers are different. The register read is in D. The register write is in W, 3 stages later.

61
00:07:06,567 --> 00:07:13,440
That is why data hazards occur through registers, and not through memory. The data memory reads and writes are both in M.

62
00:07:13,890 --> 00:07:23,178
Slide 23 gives three approaches. The first is interlock. The hardware blocks the dependent instruction, the subtract, until the hazard clears.

63
00:07:23,628 --> 00:07:27,715
Without forwarding, the add writes x1 in W, in cycle 5.

64
00:07:28,165 --> 00:07:32,623
The subtract must read x1 in D after the write: in cycle 6.

65
00:07:33,073 --> 00:07:38,971
So the subtract repeats D three times: D star in cycles 3, 4 and 5.

66
00:07:39,421 --> 00:07:45,969
The second approach is bypass, or forwarding. The value goes straight to the instruction that needs it.

67
00:07:46,419 --> 00:07:49,902
The add has its result at the end of X, in cycle 3.

68
00:07:50,352 --> 00:07:57,039
The hardware sends x1 straight to the subtract in X, in cycle 4. So the subtract does not stall.

69
00:07:57,489 --> 00:08:06,731
The third approach is speculation: guess the value, and correct the work if the guess was wrong. This video uses interlock and bypass.

70
00:08:07,181 --> 00:08:13,450
Slide 28 makes an observation. The add computes x3 at the end of X, in cycle 3.

71
00:08:13,900 --> 00:08:18,405
But it writes x3 into the register file only in W, in cycle 5.

72
00:08:18,855 --> 00:08:24,381
Follow the value. In cycle 4, x3 is in the X slash M register.

73
00:08:24,831 --> 00:08:28,779
In cycle 5, x3 is in the M slash W register.

74
00:08:29,229 --> 00:08:31,736
From cycle 6, x3 is in the register file.

75
00:08:32,186 --> 00:08:40,081
The load word reads the wrong x3 in D, in cycle 3. But it uses x3 only at the start of X, in cycle 4.

76
00:08:40,531 --> 00:08:45,872
In cycle 4, x3 is in X slash M. Taking it from there is an MX forward.

77
00:08:46,322 --> 00:08:50,223
The add immediate uses x3 in X, in cycle 5.

78
00:08:50,673 --> 00:08:56,710
In cycle 5, x3 is in M slash W. Taking it from there is a WX forward.

79
00:08:57,160 --> 00:09:01,479
So forwarding reads a value from a pipeline register, not from the register file.

80
00:09:01,929 --> 00:09:08,988
Here is the pipelined datapath from video 8. The new parts are the two muxes in front of the ALU, ringed in orange.

81
00:09:09,438 --> 00:09:18,633
Each mux selects the register value from D slash X, or a value from a later pipeline register: X slash M, or M slash W.

82
00:09:19,083 --> 00:09:26,513
The MX path takes the ALU result from X slash M back to the first ALU input, mux A, as on slide 29.

83
00:09:26,963 --> 00:09:35,699
The WX path takes the value from M slash W back to mux A, as on slide 31. It can be an ALU result or loaded data.

84
00:09:36,149 --> 00:09:48,600
Slides 32 to 34 look at the second ALU input, mux B. An R-type instruction sends rs2 to the ALU. So mux B also gets the MX and WX paths.

85
00:09:49,050 --> 00:10:00,521
Slides 35 to 38 add the WM path. It goes from M slash W to the store-data input of the data memory, DataW. A store needs its data only at the start of M.

86
00:10:00,971 --> 00:10:07,380
WM does not go to the address input of the data memory. The ALU computes the address earlier, in X.

87
00:10:07,830 --> 00:10:11,591
In the course datapath, store data has only the WM path.

88
00:10:12,041 --> 00:10:17,754
Here is the MX forward of slide 40. In cycle 3, the add is in X. It computes x1.

89
00:10:18,204 --> 00:10:22,569
The subtract is in D. It reads the old x1 from the register file.

90
00:10:23,019 --> 00:10:28,963
In cycle 4, the add is in M. So x1 is in X slash M.

91
00:10:29,413 --> 00:10:35,079
The subtract is in X. Its mux A takes x1 from X slash M, by the MX path.

92
00:10:35,529 --> 00:10:44,120
In the pipeline diagram, the green arrow goes from M to X. The from stage and the to stage are in the same cycle, cycle 4.

93
00:10:44,570 --> 00:10:47,728
So with MX forwarding, the subtract does not stall.

94
00:10:48,178 --> 00:10:56,027
Now the WX forward of slide 40. A load word is between the add and the subtract. The load does not use x1.

95
00:10:56,477 --> 00:11:01,538
In cycle 4, the subtract is in D. It reads the old x1 from the register file.

96
00:11:01,988 --> 00:11:07,003
In cycle 5, the add is in W. So x1 is in M slash W.

97
00:11:07,453 --> 00:11:13,630
The subtract is in X. Its mux A takes x1 from M slash W, by the WX path.

98
00:11:14,080 --> 00:11:24,668
In the diagram, the blue arrow goes from W to X, in cycle 5. So an instruction two places after an ALU producer gets the value by WX, with no stall.

99
00:11:25,118 --> 00:11:35,103
Now the WM forward of slide 40. The store word stores x1, the result of the add. So x1 is the store data, not the address.

100
00:11:35,553 --> 00:11:42,333
In cycle 4, the store is in X. The ALU computes its address. The store does not need x1 yet.

101
00:11:42,783 --> 00:11:47,798
In cycle 5, the add is in W. So x1 is in M slash W.

102
00:11:48,248 --> 00:11:55,725
The store is in M, and needs the data now. The WM path sends x1 into the data input of the memory, DataW.

103
00:11:56,175 --> 00:12:02,026
So the store reads its data at the start of M. Its address register, x5, needs no forward.

104
00:12:02,476 --> 00:12:14,690
To understand stores, look at what a store does with its two registers. Store word x 3, 0 of x 4, writes x 3 into memory, at address x 4 plus 0.

105
00:12:15,140 --> 00:12:18,948
The register rs1, here x 4, gives the address.

106
00:12:19,398 --> 00:12:31,565
The address goes into the ALU in X. So the store needs rs1 at the start of X. The ALU input has a mux, so it can take x 4 by MX or by WX.

107
00:12:32,015 --> 00:12:36,055
The register rs2, here x 3, is the store data.

108
00:12:36,505 --> 00:12:44,106
It goes into the data input of the memory, DataW, in M. So the store needs rs2 later, at the start of M.

109
00:12:44,556 --> 00:12:53,054
In X, rs2 passes from D slash X to X slash M with no mux. So its only forwarding path is WM.

110
00:12:53,504 --> 00:13:03,275
Now the producer side. An ALU instruction, like add, has its result at the end of X. Then the value moves to X slash M, and then to M slash W.

111
00:13:03,725 --> 00:13:08,926
A load has its value only at the end of M. Then the value moves to M slash W.

112
00:13:09,376 --> 00:13:17,085
So a forward works only if two things are true in the cycle of the need. First, the value is in a pipeline register.

113
00:13:17,535 --> 00:13:23,387
Second, a path goes from that register to the input that needs it. Now look at three cases.

114
00:13:23,837 --> 00:13:31,035
Case 1. The load writes x 3. The store writes x 3 into memory. So x 3 is the store data.

115
00:13:31,485 --> 00:13:39,612
In cycle 3, the store is in D. It reads the old x 3. That is not a problem, because the store does not use x 3 before M.

116
00:13:40,062 --> 00:13:46,842
In cycle 4, the load is in M. It reads the memory, so x 3 is ready at the end of cycle 4. The green ring marks it.

117
00:13:47,292 --> 00:13:52,633
The store is in X. It computes its address from x 4. It does not need x 3 yet.

118
00:13:53,083 --> 00:13:58,096
In cycle 5, the load is in W. So x 3 is in M slash W.

119
00:13:58,546 --> 00:14:04,305
The store is in M, and needs x 3 now. The orange ring marks it.

120
00:14:04,755 --> 00:14:09,770
The WM path sends x 3 from M slash W to the data input of the memory.

121
00:14:10,220 --> 00:14:16,675
Ready in cycle 4, needed in cycle 5. The need comes after the value exists. So there is no stall.

122
00:14:17,125 --> 00:14:21,305
Case 2. Now the store uses x 3 as its address.

123
00:14:21,755 --> 00:14:30,346
Without a stall, the store would be in X in cycle 4. The address goes into the ALU. So the store needs x 3 at the start of cycle 4.

124
00:14:30,796 --> 00:14:37,716
But in cycle 4, the load is in M. The value x 3 is ready only at the end of cycle 4.

125
00:14:38,166 --> 00:14:42,438
The need comes before the value exists. No forwarding path can fix that.

126
00:14:42,888 --> 00:14:48,182
So the store stalls for one cycle. In cycle 3, it repeats D: a D star.

127
00:14:48,632 --> 00:14:53,646
In cycle 5, the load is in W. So x 3 is in M slash W.

128
00:14:54,096 --> 00:15:01,480
The store is in X, and needs x 3 at the start of X. The ALU mux takes x 3 by WX.

129
00:15:01,930 --> 00:15:08,385
So the cost is one stall: one D star. It is the load-use case, which comes later in this video.

130
00:15:08,835 --> 00:15:17,891
Case 3. The add writes x 1. The store word, two instructions later, stores x 1. So x 1 is store data.

131
00:15:18,341 --> 00:15:24,331
The diagram shows the timing without stalls. Look for a cycle where a path can bring x 1 to the store.

132
00:15:24,781 --> 00:15:30,656
Cycle 4. The store is in D. The add is in M, so x 1 is in X slash M.

133
00:15:31,106 --> 00:15:36,633
But no path goes into D. So the store reads the old x 1 from the register file.

134
00:15:37,083 --> 00:15:43,724
Cycle 5. The store is in X. The add is in W, so x 1 is in M slash W.

135
00:15:44,174 --> 00:15:52,161
The WX path goes only to the ALU inputs. The store data passes X with no mux. So WX cannot help.

136
00:15:52,611 --> 00:16:01,549
Cycle 6. The store is in M, where WM could help. But the add has left the pipeline. x 1 is now only in the register file.

137
00:16:01,999 --> 00:16:08,129
WM takes the value from M slash W, and the add is not there. So WM is too late.

138
00:16:08,579 --> 00:16:17,449
So no path reaches the store data. The store must read x 1 from the register file in D, after the add writes it in W, in cycle 5.

139
00:16:17,899 --> 00:16:23,101
So the store is in D in cycle 6, after two D stars, in cycles 4 and 5.

140
00:16:23,551 --> 00:16:30,006
The value was ready early, at the end of X in cycle 3. But no path could deliver it.

141
00:16:30,456 --> 00:16:41,416
Here are the three cases together. Case 1: the loaded value is store data. It is ready at the end of M in cycle 4. It is needed at the start of M in cycle 5.

142
00:16:41,866 --> 00:16:47,485
Cycle 5 comes after cycle 4. So WM forwards the value, with no stall.

143
00:16:47,935 --> 00:16:56,248
Case 2: the loaded value is the address. It is ready at the end of M in cycle 4. It is needed at the start of X, also in cycle 4.

144
00:16:56,698 --> 00:17:02,270
The need comes before the value exists. So there is one stall, then WX.

145
00:17:02,720 --> 00:17:13,216
Case 3: store data from two instructions ahead. It is ready early, at the end of X in cycle 3. Without stalls, it is needed at the start of M in cycle 6.

146
00:17:13,666 --> 00:17:22,954
But no path reaches the store data. So the store reads the value in D in cycle 6, after the producer writes it. That costs two stalls.

147
00:17:23,404 --> 00:17:31,391
The course rule from slides 38 and 39: store data uses only WM, because rs2 passes X with no mux.

148
00:17:31,841 --> 00:17:36,886
If WM is too late, the store reads the value in D, after the producer writes it in W.

149
00:17:37,336 --> 00:17:44,163
Fall 2019 question 2 part B asks you to add a forwarding path that removes these stalls.

150
00:17:44,613 --> 00:17:55,712
A WX path into the store data does it. In case 3, the store then gets x 1 by WX in cycle 5, with no stall. Video 10 solves this question.

151
00:17:56,162 --> 00:18:05,357
Slide 39 adds forwarding control logic. It sets the select input of each ALU mux. For mux A, the select input is ASel.

152
00:18:05,807 --> 00:18:11,287
Input 0 of mux A is the ALU result in X slash M: the MX path.

153
00:18:11,737 --> 00:18:15,917
Input 1 is the value in M slash W: the WX path.

154
00:18:16,367 --> 00:18:20,453
The other inputs take the register value from D slash X, or the pc.

155
00:18:20,903 --> 00:18:28,334
Now the logic for mux A. If rs1 in D slash X equals rd in X slash M, select input 0, the MX path.

156
00:18:28,784 --> 00:18:36,075
If rs1 in D slash X equals rd in M slash W, select input 1, the WX path.

157
00:18:36,525 --> 00:18:45,116
Slide 40 gives an example, in cycle 4. The subtract is in X, so it is in D slash X. Its rs1 is x1.

158
00:18:45,566 --> 00:18:52,764
The add is in X slash M. Its rd is x1. rs1 and rd match, so ASel is 0.

159
00:18:53,214 --> 00:19:00,420
Each mux has its own logic. The mux of the second ALU input compares rs2 in the same way.

160
00:19:00,870 --> 00:19:12,898
A check question, with three instructions. The add and the add immediate both write x1. The subtract reads x1. Find what mux A selects in cycle 5. Pause the video and try it.

161
00:19:13,348 --> 00:19:18,410
In cycle 5, the subtract is in D slash X. Its rs1 is x1.

162
00:19:18,860 --> 00:19:24,712
X slash M holds the add immediate. Its rd is x1, so it matches rs1.

163
00:19:25,162 --> 00:19:31,093
M slash W holds the add. Its rd is also x1, so it also matches rs1.

164
00:19:31,543 --> 00:19:40,088
Both match. Pick X slash M, input 0, so ASel is 0. The add immediate is younger, so it is the most recent writer of x1.

165
00:19:40,538 --> 00:19:51,219
One more case. The consumer, the subtract, is instruction 4. The load is instruction 1. So the subtract is three instructions after the load.

166
00:19:51,669 --> 00:19:55,152
The load writes x1 in W, in cycle 5.

167
00:19:55,602 --> 00:19:59,457
The subtract is in D in the same cycle, cycle 5.

168
00:19:59,907 --> 00:20:07,802
D sees only values written in earlier cycles, and there is no forwarding into D. So the subtract reads the old x1.

169
00:20:08,252 --> 00:20:12,478
So the subtract repeats D once: a D star in cycle 5.

170
00:20:12,928 --> 00:20:19,476
In cycle 6, it reads the new x1. The lab note on slide 60 calls this case the WD hazard.

171
00:20:19,926 --> 00:20:25,034
Fall 2019 question 2 states this rule: no forwarding into the decode stage.

172
00:20:25,484 --> 00:20:34,679
Some old quizzes assume that the register file writes first and reads second in one cycle. Then there is no stall. State your assumption in the exam.

173
00:20:35,129 --> 00:20:42,281
This table collects the rules, by the distance d. The consumer is d instructions after its producer.

174
00:20:42,731 --> 00:20:48,257
For an ALU result used by the ALU, at distance 1: MX, no stall.

175
00:20:48,707 --> 00:20:52,005
At distance 2: WX, no stall.

176
00:20:52,455 --> 00:20:55,566
At distance 3: the WD case, one stall.

177
00:20:56,016 --> 00:21:03,446
For a load result used by the ALU, at distance 1: one stall, then WX. It is the load-use case.

178
00:21:03,896 --> 00:21:07,194
At distance 2: WX, no stall.

179
00:21:07,644 --> 00:21:10,755
At distance 3: the WD case, one stall.

180
00:21:11,205 --> 00:21:16,546
For an ALU result used as store data, at distance 1: WM, no stall.

181
00:21:16,996 --> 00:21:21,315
At distance 2: two stalls, because store data has only WM.

182
00:21:21,765 --> 00:21:24,876
At distance 3: the WD case, one stall.

183
00:21:25,326 --> 00:21:29,877
At distance 4 or more, the consumer reads the value from the register file. No stall.

184
00:21:30,327 --> 00:21:36,364
To read a diagram, find the cycle where the consumer needs the value: its X, or its M for store data.

185
00:21:36,814 --> 00:21:44,338
Look at the producer in that column. M means MX. W means WX, or WM for store data.

186
00:21:44,788 --> 00:21:51,661
Also, a load followed by a store of the loaded value, at distance 1, uses WM with no stall.

187
00:21:52,111 --> 00:22:03,781
Now practice the exam format with the sequence of slide 24. Full forwarding, no forwarding into D. Draw the diagram, and fill both tables. Pause the video and try it.

188
00:22:04,231 --> 00:22:09,479
Cycle 4. The load word is in X. It needs x3 for its address.

189
00:22:09,929 --> 00:22:15,865
The add is in M. So x3 goes from X slash M to the load: an MX forward.

190
00:22:16,315 --> 00:22:23,049
Cycle 5. The add immediate is in X, and the add is in W. So x3 goes by WX.

191
00:22:23,499 --> 00:22:29,954
The store word is in D in the add's W cycle. That is the WD case, so the store repeats D.

192
00:22:30,404 --> 00:22:34,723
The store reads x3 in D in cycle 6, from the register file.

193
00:22:35,173 --> 00:22:37,281
The program ends in cycle 9.

194
00:22:37,731 --> 00:22:48,087
The forwarding table has one row per forward. Row 1: source 1, destination 2, register x3, from M to X, in cycle 4.

195
00:22:48,537 --> 00:22:56,339
Row 2: source 1, destination 3, register x3, from W to X, in cycle 5.

196
00:22:56,789 --> 00:23:03,941
The stall table has one row. Instruction 4 stalls in D in cycle 5. The reason is the WD case.

197
00:23:04,391 --> 00:23:15,395
One more practice: a load, a store of the loaded value, an add, and a subtract. Use the same rules. Pause the video and try it.

198
00:23:15,845 --> 00:23:26,108
Cycle 5. The load is in W. The store is in M, and needs x5 as store data. So x5 goes by WM.

199
00:23:26,558 --> 00:23:31,434
The add is in X. It also needs x5 from the load. So x5 goes by WX.

200
00:23:31,884 --> 00:23:37,597
The subtract is in D in the load's W cycle. So it stalls: the WD case.

201
00:23:38,047 --> 00:23:47,335
The subtract also needs x6 from the add. Without the stall, the subtract would be in X in cycle 6, with the add in M. That would be MX.

202
00:23:47,785 --> 00:23:55,819
With the stall, the subtract is in X in cycle 7, and the add is in W. So x6 goes by WX.

203
00:23:56,269 --> 00:23:58,376
The program ends in cycle 9.

204
00:23:58,826 --> 00:24:06,953
The forwarding table has three rows. Row 1: x5 goes from instruction 1 to instruction 2, from W to M, in cycle 5.

205
00:24:07,403 --> 00:24:14,741
Row 2: x5 also goes from instruction 1 to instruction 3, from W to X, in cycle 5.

206
00:24:15,191 --> 00:24:22,064
Row 3: x6 goes from instruction 3 to instruction 4, from W to X, in cycle 7.

207
00:24:22,514 --> 00:24:28,830
The stall table has one row. Instruction 4 stalls in D in cycle 5: the WD case.

208
00:24:29,280 --> 00:24:33,738
The lesson: a stall can change the forwarding path. So draw the stalls first.

209
00:24:34,188 --> 00:24:40,179
Some data hazards remain. Slide 43 shows a load, and an add that uses its result, x3.

210
00:24:40,629 --> 00:24:43,972
The load has its value at the end of M, in cycle 4.

211
00:24:44,422 --> 00:24:50,367
The add needs x3 at the start of X, also in cycle 4. The value comes too late.

212
00:24:50,817 --> 00:24:57,504
So the hardware detects the load-use case, and injects one stall. In cycle 3, the add repeats D.

213
00:24:57,954 --> 00:24:59,904
The subtract behind it repeats F.

214
00:25:00,354 --> 00:25:05,649
In cycle 4, the add is in D again. A bubble, which does nothing, is in X.

215
00:25:06,099 --> 00:25:13,441
In cycle 5, the load is in W, and the add is in X. Now WX forwards the loaded value.

216
00:25:13,891 --> 00:25:20,531
Slide 47 shows the result. The load-use case costs one stall cycle, then a WX forward.

217
00:25:20,981 --> 00:25:28,086
Now the stall hardware, with the load-use example of slide 47. In cycle 3, the load is in X.

218
00:25:28,536 --> 00:25:32,391
The add is in D. It needs x3, the result of the load.

219
00:25:32,841 --> 00:25:34,095
The subtract is in F.

220
00:25:34,545 --> 00:25:38,910
At the end of cycle 3, the hardware writes a nop into D slash X.

221
00:25:39,360 --> 00:25:42,890
So in cycle 4, a bubble is in X. It does nothing.

222
00:25:43,340 --> 00:25:47,453
The hardware also disables the writes of F slash D and of the PC.

223
00:25:47,903 --> 00:25:54,272
So in cycle 4, F slash D still holds the add, and the PC still points to the subtract.

224
00:25:54,722 --> 00:26:00,806
In cycle 5, the add is in X. It gets x3 from the load in W, by WX.

225
00:26:01,256 --> 00:26:06,874
Slide 49 gives the stall condition. First, the instruction in D slash X must be a load.

226
00:26:07,324 --> 00:26:12,232
Second, its rd equals rs1 of the instruction in F slash D.

227
00:26:12,682 --> 00:26:22,481
Or its rd equals rs2 of that instruction, and that instruction is not a store. A store does not stall for rs2: WM forwards store data in time.

228
00:26:22,931 --> 00:26:28,736
Now check cycle 3. D slash X holds the load. It is a load, with rd x3.

229
00:26:29,186 --> 00:26:34,062
F slash D holds the add. Its rs1 is x2, which is not x3.

230
00:26:34,512 --> 00:26:40,270
Its rs2 is x3, which equals rd. And the add is not a store. So Stall is 1.

231
00:26:40,720 --> 00:26:47,919
Slide 52 calculates the cost of load-use stalls. 20 percent of the instructions are loads.

232
00:26:48,369 --> 00:26:54,499
Half of the loads, 50 percent, are followed by a dependent instruction. That costs a 1-cycle stall.

233
00:26:54,949 --> 00:26:59,639
All branches are not taken. Find the CPI. Pause the video and try it.

234
00:27:00,089 --> 00:27:02,777
Look at 10 instructions, numbered 1 to 10.

235
00:27:03,227 --> 00:27:05,967
2 of the 10 are loads: 20 percent.

236
00:27:06,417 --> 00:27:16,913
Instruction 3 uses the result of load 2. Instruction 8 does not use load 7. So 1 of the 2 loads, 50 percent, has a dependent next instruction.

237
00:27:17,363 --> 00:27:20,567
That costs one bubble: a 1-cycle stall.

238
00:27:21,017 --> 00:27:25,289
Each box takes one cycle. So the 10 instructions take 11 cycles.

239
00:27:25,739 --> 00:27:31,637
The CPI is cycles divided by instructions: 11 divided by 10, which is 1.1.

240
00:27:32,087 --> 00:27:41,840
Slide 52 uses a formula. The CPI is 1, plus the stall cycles, times the fraction of loads, times the fraction of loads with a dependent next instruction.

241
00:27:42,290 --> 00:27:44,983
The first 1 is one cycle per instruction.

242
00:27:45,433 --> 00:27:48,452
The second 1 is the bubble: 1 stall cycle.

243
00:27:48,902 --> 00:27:50,992
20 percent is 2 loads of 10.

244
00:27:51,442 --> 00:27:54,089
50 percent is 1 of the 2 loads.

245
00:27:54,539 --> 00:28:04,616
1 times point 2 times point 5 is point 1. So the CPI is 1 plus point 1, which is 1.1: the same result as the count.

246
00:28:05,066 --> 00:28:12,444
In general, the CPI is 1, plus the stall cycles times their fractions. Reducing load-use stalls is important.

247
00:28:12,894 --> 00:28:20,510
Slide 53 adds a 4-cycle multiply. Its 4 stages, P0 to P3, take the place of X and M.

248
00:28:20,960 --> 00:28:27,369
Separate pipeline registers connect the multiplier to W. A state machine controls the pipeline.

249
00:28:27,819 --> 00:28:30,768
The add immediate needs x4 from the multiply.

250
00:28:31,218 --> 00:28:34,933
The product exists at the end of P3, in cycle 6.

251
00:28:35,383 --> 00:28:39,934
So the add immediate waits three cycles in D: D star in cycles 3, 4 and 5.

252
00:28:40,384 --> 00:28:46,839
In cycle 7, the multiply is in W, and the add immediate is in X. WX forwards x4.

253
00:28:47,289 --> 00:28:54,488
The multiplier is often pipelined. Its registers and ALUs are replicated, so a new multiply can start in each cycle.

254
00:28:54,938 --> 00:29:00,325
Slide 55 shows what can go wrong. The add immediate and the add do not depend on the multiply.

255
00:29:00,775 --> 00:29:04,536
But the add reaches W in cycle 7, together with the multiply.

256
00:29:04,986 --> 00:29:10,373
Both write the register file in cycle 7: a structural hazard on the single write port.

257
00:29:10,823 --> 00:29:16,520
Also, the younger add immediate finishes in cycle 6, before the older multiply in cycle 7.

258
00:29:16,970 --> 00:29:23,100
Slides 56 and 57 show a worse case. The multiply and the add immediate both write x4.

259
00:29:23,550 --> 00:29:31,117
The add immediate writes x4 in W, in cycle 6. The older multiply writes x4 in cycle 7.

260
00:29:31,567 --> 00:29:35,978
So the writes to x4 are in the wrong order: a WAW hazard.

261
00:29:36,428 --> 00:29:46,181
The add reads x4 in D, in cycle 8. It should get x4 from the add immediate, but it gets the product. Deeper pipelines make this common.

262
00:29:46,631 --> 00:29:53,179
Slide 58 shows the corrected diagram. Its stall logic prevents mis-ordered writes to the same register, x4.

263
00:29:53,629 --> 00:29:59,109
The add immediate now waits two cycles in D: D star in cycles 3 and 4.

264
00:29:59,559 --> 00:30:07,546
So its W is in cycle 8, after the multiply's W in cycle 7. The writes to x4 are now in program order.

265
00:30:07,996 --> 00:30:13,987
Now look at the add. In cycle 8, it is in D. That is the W cycle of the add immediate.

266
00:30:14,437 --> 00:30:20,428
It is the WD case. So the add repeats D, and reads x4 in cycle 9.

267
00:30:20,878 --> 00:30:25,522
Slide 60 is a note for the lab. Add bypass paths to the branch compare unit.

268
00:30:25,972 --> 00:30:30,802
When several bypass paths are enabled, pick the correct one: the most recent writer.

269
00:30:31,252 --> 00:30:35,571
Consider the WD structural hazard. The lab work itself is yours.

270
00:30:36,021 --> 00:30:49,537
Here is the recap. One. For a pair a b, with a older: RAW means b reads what a wrote. WAR means b writes what a reads. WAW means both write.

271
00:30:49,987 --> 00:30:55,136
Two. A structural hazard means that two instructions need one resource in one cycle.

272
00:30:55,586 --> 00:31:02,095
Three. An ALU value exists at the end of X. A load value exists at the end of M.

273
00:31:02,545 --> 00:31:08,162
Four. MX and WX feed the ALU. WM feeds only store data.

274
00:31:08,612 --> 00:31:14,797
Five. There is no forwarding into D. A consumer in D in its producer's W cycle repeats D.

275
00:31:15,247 --> 00:31:25,350
Six. A load-use costs one stall, then WX. The CPI is 1 plus the stall cycles times their fractions.

276
00:31:25,800 --> 00:31:31,691
Seven. Store data from two instructions ahead costs two stalls. Draw the stalls first.

277
00:31:32,141 --> 00:31:39,028
Eight. With multi-cycle units, stall so that register writes stay in order. That is the end of video 9.
